 global national  as shots begin to reach remote first  nations across canada  indigenous leaders are encouraging their  communities to get vaccinated  as of friday indigenous services canada  had confirmed nearly 10  000 cases of covet 19 in first nations  communities across the country  but chiefs say some members are hesitant  to get the shot because of  discrimination  and distrust in the health care system  and that's not the only barrier facing  indigenous communities fighting the  virus  there's often a lack of information for  those who speak different languages  but as linda ailsworth reports a bc  medical student is helping to change  that  there's been a lot of information to  take in since the start of the kovit 19  pandemic  and a lot of changes to adapt to one of  the main things that people have had to  adapt to is  mass the second thing has to be about  social distancing  and the last thing about hand hygiene  but what if you were an indigenous  person who wasn't privy to that  information  because it wasn't available in your  native language a lot of the times  marginalized populations are left behind  and i think kobe 19 is a perfect example  to exemplify that  where indigenous communities across the  world are disproportionately being  affected by kova 19.  suk meets satchel a second year  university of bc medical student wasn't  the only one who was concerned  so was victor carmen a harvard student  on the other side of the continent  as an indigenous medical student i  felt like i was in a position where i  could do some good  it was on facebook that the two found  each other in combined forces  with a few other like-minded students to  make a difference  so one of the things we came up with was  translations for our nations where  we provide information about kobe 19  in indigenous languages they went about  recruiting over a hundred indigenous  speaking people in 40 countries to  translate fact sheets  then created a website that made it all  available free of charge  when we go to translations these are all  the different  indigenous languages that we have among  the many languages they've translated  samburu in northern kenya one of them  for kobe 19 information for indigenous  children and youth  and something like this which is very  visual the team was thrilled to get this  photo of the semburu children looking at  the material for the first time  everyone there was so receptive and they  really appreciated the efforts that were  being taken  the project has been such a success that  victor and sukmeet hope to roll out  information on the kovid 19 vaccine next  and perhaps even meet one day we talk  all the time about what we can do  together  to really make a difference in this  world linda ailsworth global news  up next what a major business deal will  mean for the legacy of one of canada's  iconic  musicians  to another music legend now they're some  of the best loved songs in this country  and around the world and  soon you may be hearing them on  commercials pitching everything from  cars to insurance neil young has sold  a half stake in his songs to an  investment company  that specializes in combining popular  music with advertising  as ross lord reports it's left his fans  wondering  what these massive business deals will  mean for youngs and  other artists legacies  [Music]  as a legendary singer-songwriter neil  young has a reputation  for protecting how his music is used  [Music]  but young songs will be connected to  commercial products  according to fellow canadian merck  mercuratis  he owns an investment company hypnosis  that just bought a 50  stake in young's catalog that's more  than 1 000 songs so we're able to put  more time and effort  into putting the songs in movies tv  commercials  making sure that they're on all the  right playlists on spotify and apple  mercuratis says he's been a neil young  fan since he was a child he says young  is at a point in his life where he's  wondering what will become of the songs  after he's gone  using songs to sell other products isn't  new  remember this bank of montreal  commercial from 1996  [Music]  to this day there's outrage that bob  dylan allowed a classic protest song to  be used in a bank promotion  i think we should fight against this  like like the times they are changing  these are counter culture songs against  the man  commercial songs are the man like it's  time  they are a change last month dylan sold  publishing rights to his entire catalog  more than 600 songs for between 300  million and 500 million dollars  stevie nicks of fleetwood mac fame  recently sold her music  for a reported 100 million dollars  young's deal  includes more than a thousand songs  reportedly for 150 million  although he keeps 50 ownership my job is  to bring neil  the best opportunities possible but the  the decisions will be ultimately being  made by neil as to whether or not  something is  is you know is done with the song on the  receiving end of those decisions  are fans like this former music reviewer  so if you suddenly hear a song that  helped define  you and your personality and how you've  lived and suddenly  it's selling motor oil  might be good if you're a car fan but if  you're not a car fan and you might be a  little bit offended he says it's unclear  if  song sell-offs are good for fans or if  the only winners are the artists and  businesses making the transactions  ross lord global news  that is global national for this sunday  i'm colleen christie  tonight's your canada is gimli manitoba  show us your part of the country  email your photos to viewers at  globalnational.com  thanks for watching donna friesen will  be back at the desk tomorrow  have a good night  you
 

